<h2>Why 7 Days Is the Perfect Amount of Time for Seoul</h2>
<p>Seoul is one of the world's great megacities — a place where ancient palaces sit next to gleaming skyscrapers, where street-food alleys disappear into Michelin-starred restaurant districts, and where you can hike a mountain in the morning and go clubbing till sunrise. Seven days gives you enough time to breathe, explore, get lost, and still hit all the highlights without burning out.</p>

<p>This itinerary is structured to minimize backtracking by grouping nearby neighborhoods together each day. It's designed for first-timers but includes plenty of local tips to keep it interesting even if you've been before.</p>

<div class="tip-box">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Buy a T-money card at the airport the moment you arrive. It works on the subway, buses, and even taxis. Top it up at any convenience store (CU, GS25, 7-Eleven). A single subway ride costs around ₩1,400–1,600 (about $1 USD).</div>

<h2>Day 1: Arrival & Myeongdong</h2>
<p>You'll likely land at Incheon International Airport (ICN). Take the AREX express train directly to Seoul Station — it takes 43 minutes and costs ₩9,500. Skip the taxi; it'll take just as long in traffic and cost ten times more.</p>

<p>After checking in, spend the afternoon in <strong>Myeongdong</strong>, Seoul's most tourist-friendly neighborhood. The pedestrian street fills up by evening with vendors selling tteokbokki (spicy rice cakes), hotteok (sweet pancakes), and tornado potatoes. Duck into any of the skincare shops — Olive Young, Innisfree, Etude House — and stock up. Prices here are the same as everywhere else, but the variety is unbeatable.</p>

<h3>Where to Eat on Day 1</h3>
<p>For dinner, head to <strong>Myeongdong Kyoja</strong> (명동교자) on the main street — it's a Seoul institution famous for kalguksu (knife-cut noodle soup) and mandu (dumplings). Expect a short queue; it moves fast. Two people can eat for around ₩20,000.</p>

<h2>Day 2: Gyeongbokgung Palace & Bukchon</h2>
<p>Start early — <strong>Gyeongbokgung Palace</strong> opens at 9am and gets crowded by 11am. Admission is ₩3,000. If you rent a hanbok (traditional dress) from one of the shops on the north side, entry is free. The palace grounds are enormous; budget at least two hours. The Changing of the Royal Guard ceremony happens at 10am and 2pm.</p>

<p>After the palace, walk north through <strong>Bukchon Hanok Village</strong> — a hillside neighborhood of 600+ traditional wooden homes. The view from the top of Bukchon-ro 11-gil looking down over the tiled rooftops toward Namsan Tower is one of the best in the city. Note: people actually live here, so be respectful of noise levels.</p>

<h3>Afternoon: Insadong</h3>
<p>Wind down the hill into <strong>Insadong</strong>, Seoul's arts and crafts district. The main street is lined with galleries, tea houses, and shops selling handmade ceramics, calligraphy supplies, and quirky gifts. Try a traditional makgeolli (rice wine) at one of the pojangmacha (street stalls).</p>

<div class="tip-box">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Gyeongbokgung is closed on Tuesdays. If you're visiting on a Tuesday, swap Day 2 and Day 3 in your itinerary.</div>

<h2>Day 3: Hongdae & Sinchon</h2>
<p><strong>Hongdae</strong> (short for Hongik University area) is Seoul's youth culture hub. During the day, browse the indie boutiques and record shops on the side streets. The weekend flea market in Hongdae Free Market runs 1pm–6pm on Saturdays and showcases local artists and designers.</p>

<p>The neighborhood transforms dramatically after dark. Street performers take over the main plaza, and the clubs and bars fill up around midnight. Clubs like <strong>Soap</strong>, <strong>VURT</strong>, and <strong>Club FF</strong> are popular. Cover charges range from ₩10,000–20,000 and usually include one drink.</p>

<h2>Day 4: Gangnam & COEX</h2>
<p>Cross the Han River to <strong>Gangnam</strong>, the upscale district that Psy made famous. The <strong>COEX Mall</strong> is a massive underground shopping complex with the stunning <strong>Starfield Library</strong> — floor-to-ceiling bookshelves that make for incredible photos. It's free to enter.</p>

<p>Walk down <strong>Garosu-gil</strong> (tree-lined street) for boutique shopping and some of the best café-hopping in the city. For dinner, explore the restaurant alleys behind Sinnonhyeon station — you'll find everything from Japanese ramen to upscale Korean barbecue.</p>

<h2>Day 5: Day Trip to the DMZ</h2>
<p>No Seoul trip is complete without visiting the <strong>Demilitarized Zone (DMZ)</strong>, the border between North and South Korea. Book a guided tour — independent visits are not allowed. Most tours depart from Dongdaemun or City Hall area at 8am and return by 5pm.</p>

<p>The <strong>Third Infiltration Tunnel</strong>, <strong>Dora Observatory</strong>, and <strong>Dorasan Station</strong> (the last train station before the border) are standard stops. It's a sobering but genuinely fascinating experience.</p>

<h2>Day 6: Itaewon & Namsan Tower</h2>
<p><strong>Itaewon</strong> is Seoul's most international neighborhood — the result of decades of proximity to the US military base. It's the best place in the city for non-Korean food: Lebanese, Ethiopian, Mexican, craft beer bars. The <strong>Leeum Samsung Museum of Art</strong> is nearby and houses an exceptional collection of both traditional Korean art and contemporary international works (admission around ₩10,000).</p>

<p>In the afternoon, take the cable car up to <strong>N Seoul Tower</strong> (₩9,500 round trip). The view over the city is especially dramatic at dusk. The "love locks" fence at the top is hopelessly romantic and very Instagrammable.</p>

