<h2>Day 7: Dongdaemun & Farewell</h2>
<p><strong>Dongdaemun Design Plaza (DDP)</strong>, designed by Zaha Hadid, is one of the most photogenic buildings in Asia — a massive silver blob rising out of the city. The adjacent fashion market is open 24 hours (best visited after 10pm when wholesale buyers show up). Grab last-minute gifts at the surrounding markets.</p>

<p>Spend your final afternoon at <strong>Cheonggyecheon Stream</strong>, a 6km urban waterway that was restored in 2005 after being buried under a highway for decades. Walk the banks south toward City Hall for a peaceful end to your trip.</p>

<h3>Practical Info</h3>
<p><strong>Budget:</strong> Expect to spend ₩80,000–120,000/day (roughly $60–90 USD) covering accommodation, food, transport, and activities if you're being moderately frugal. Seoul is surprisingly affordable for a world-class city.</p>
<p><strong>Language:</strong> Google Translate's camera mode handles Korean menus perfectly. Most subway signs are in English. English-speaking restaurant staff are common in tourist areas but less so in residential neighborhoods.</p>
<p><strong>Safety:</strong> Seoul is one of the safest major cities on earth. You can walk alone at night, leave bags unattended, and generally not worry. The main concern is car traffic — look both ways, especially on smaller streets where scooters ignore signals.</p>

